# Cross-platform line-ending normalization. Without this, clones on
# Windows + macOS + Linux fight over CRLF vs LF and `git status` shows
# hundreds of tracked files as modified after the first text-file edit
# on a fresh cross-platform clone. Caught during a multi-machine Recto
# bring-up: a fresh clone on a Windows host showed ~hundreds of files
# as ` M` modified due to autocrlf flapping vs the canonical LF state
# pushed by the original author. Enforcing LF-in-tree resolves it.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: detect text files automatically (vs binary),
# and store text files with LF in the index regardless of the working-
# tree's local convention. Working-tree files on Windows still appear
# with CRLF when the user has core.autocrlf=true; the difference is
# invisible because git no longer sees a delta.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that genuinely need CRLF in the working tree (Windows-specific
# scripts that must work with the cmd.exe parser).
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell scripts: leave at LF default. Both PS5.1 and PS7 handle
# LF .ps1 files cleanly; we standardize on LF for cross-platform
# script-authoring (scripts may be written on Linux and run on Windows).

# Binary files — never normalize, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp  binary
*.ico  binary
*.svg  text
*.pdf  binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.zip  binary
*.tar  binary
*.gz   binary
*.7z   binary
*.dll  binary
*.exe  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.bundle binary
*.jks  binary
*.keystore binary
*.dpapi binary
